1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,279
Let's convert our role tables into sources

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:08,760
Sources are an abstraction on top of your input data

3
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:14,498
It gives you a few extra features just like checking the data freshness

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,549
And also as you will see later in the documentation

5
00:00:18,549 --> 00:00:21,153
sources come up as special entities

6
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:24,488
So what we are going to do here in this video

7
00:00:24,488 --> 00:00:30,061
it won't change the actual behavior of dbt or the queries it executes

8
00:00:30,061 --> 00:00:33,633
but our project will be more structured

9
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:37,060
So let's define a few sources

10
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:41,353
Sources can be defined in any YAML file in Models

11
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:44,660
I will come here and create a new YAML file

12
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:47,660
and I encourage you to do the same

13
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:54,537
This will be sources yml

14
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:57,452
And as always I will simply go and copy my code

15
00:00:57,452 --> 00:01:04,420
and you can go and check it out in the plus resources

16
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:09,914
So here I'm giving aliases to actual tables in Snowflake

17
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:13,000
This is very much what sources are about

18
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:20,280
So I can say that I create a source namespace which I call Airbnb

19
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:23,580
And the schema I'm picking data from is row

20
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:28,000
So here I define only a single database and a single schema

21
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,203
And here the tables in this database and schema will be

22
00:01:32,203 --> 00:01:35,441
row listings which I want to call listings

23
00:01:35,441 --> 00:01:38,304
row hosts which I want to call hosts

24
00:01:38,304 --> 00:01:41,920
and row reviews which I want to call reviews

25
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:45,677
Well that's very much it

26
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:52,069
So with this I added an extra abstraction on top of my row layer

27
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:56,560
And now I can reference my row tables as source tables

28
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:59,553
So let's take a look how this works in action

29
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,345
Let's go to my source tables

30
00:02:01,345 --> 00:02:04,916
because here I found those models which reference row tables

31
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:10,345
If I open row listings then you will see that here

32
00:02:10,345 --> 00:02:16,680
we use a direct reference to our Snowflake instance

33
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:19,480
right But what happens if these tables move

34
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:24,660
or you have different instances which use different schemas and so on

35
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:28,475
And this is where sources and this abstraction helps you

36
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:36,440
So using a source comes through the template tag source It's quite simple

37
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:45,400
We will pass Airbnb and then in our case listings And there we go

38
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:51,640
So now we reference instead of the direct reference to a table we reference our sources

39
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:56,538
And here you see it will look up Airbnb listings

40
00:02:56,538 --> 00:03:03,280
and then dbt will know that I'm after row row listings

41
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:05,997
So I have this here with Airbnb and listings

42
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:15,557
I can do the same with Airbnb hosts in the hosts file and also refuse

43
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:22,398
So I can go and replace this to Airbnb reviews

44
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:28,317
Okay so now I have a more structured project

45
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,198
Let's see if it works

46
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,919
I will not execute this with dbt run

47
00:03:32,919 --> 00:03:36,547
because we have another command just for checking

48
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:39,200
if all the template tags and the connections

49
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:43,320
and everything we defined in our project makes sense

50
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:49,120
And this is called dbt compile

51
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:52,500
With dbt compile dbt goes through all of your models

52
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:01,840
and YAML files and tests and so on

53
00:04:01,840 --> 00:04:07,320
And it says okay It's okay So we are good

54
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:09,296
We have our sources defined now

55
00:04:09,296 --> 00:04:11,519
and you will see in the next session

56
00:04:11,519 --> 00:04:15,320
how we can check for data staleness in sources
